+# Decision: Reconcile Concurrent Visits with Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Workers can edit the same visit independently while tablets are disconnected. A later upload must not overwrite an earlier correction merely because it arrived later. The store must preserve what each worker submitted, give coordinators a tractable way to resolve competing observations, and provide review-export with an unambiguous current record.
+
+The choice is not whether to retain original submissions; both credible approaches do so. It is whether unrelated edits should combine automatically or whether every concurrent edit should remain part of a complete visit awaiting coordinator resolution. That choice affects scientific coherence, coordinator workload before the weekly export, tablet sync data, and the provenance review tooling must present.
+
+The [record-store design](../record-store-design.md) defines the selected mechanism's operational contract.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Major — Scientific coherence:** Prefer a mechanism that cannot create a new combination of scientifically related observations without a coordinator seeing the competing visits. Species identification, cover estimates, observation dates, and notes can have relationships that a field schema does not capture.
+- **Major — Review workload and export readiness:** Keep the two coordinators' queue manageable and avoid holding visits out of the Friday CSV unnecessarily. Automatic resolution of unrelated edits directly improves this criterion.
+- **Moderate — Implementation and maintenance burden:** Favor contracts and rules that a small team can implement, test, explain, and change without dedicated systems administration. This engages the tenet **Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator**.
+- **Moderate — Explainable provenance:** Make the path from submitted observations to the current record inspectable without reconstructing hidden transformations. This engages the tenet **Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable**.
+- **Minor — Offline sync cost:** Limit tablet storage, request complexity, and transmission volume during intermittent connectivity. Local bookkeeping and upload size may pull toward different approaches.
+
+Both approaches must retain immutable original submissions and support disconnected editing; those inherited constraints do not distinguish the options.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each worker submits a complete visit and the revision ID from which the edit started. An edit based on the sole current tip replaces it. An edit based on any other historical revision creates a competing complete revision. While multiple active tips exist, the visit is unresolved and has no current revision.
+
+A coordinator reviews the complete candidates and creates a complete result revision. The result can reproduce one candidate or contain coordinator corrections, and its resolution record links every resolved candidate. No fields from different worker submissions are combined automatically.
+
+**Demonstration:** If revisions `R7` and `R8` both name `R6` as their base, both remain immutable candidates. A coordinator resolves exactly `[R7, R8]` into `R9`. Until that resolution, the visit is unresolved; afterward, `R9` is the sole current revision.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Strong — Scientific coherence:** A submitted visit remains an indivisible scientific statement until a coordinator reviews it. The mechanism does not need to know whether two field edits are related.
+- **Weak — Review workload and export readiness:** Even an observer-name correction concurrent with an added species creates an unresolved visit. Every such visit requires coordinator action before it has a current revision eligible for export.
+- **Strong — Implementation and maintenance burden:** Branch and frontier rules are independent of the scientific schema. Adding or changing scientific fields does not require a corresponding merge-rule change.
+- **Strong — Explainable provenance:** Candidates and resolution results are complete snapshots linked by revision and resolution records. Review tooling can show exactly what each worker submitted and what the coordinator produced.
+- **Weak — Offline sync cost:** Tablets retain only a base revision ID for concurrency purposes, but every edit uploads a complete visit rather than only changed fields.
+
+**Summary:** Whole-visit revisions trade additional coordinator work and full-payload uploads for scientific conservatism and a small, stable reconciliation mechanism.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each tablet retains a base snapshot and submits structured changes against it. The store compares those changes with edits accepted since the base. Changes to disjoint fields merge automatically; overlapping changes go to review. The current record carries field-level provenance so coordinators can inspect which submissions supplied each value.
+
+Merge rules must define behavior for scalar fields, observer lists, species entries, and additions or removals. Conservative rules can treat an entire species entry as one unit and route uncertain cases to review. Coordinators can also approve every automatically combined record, although doing so restores some of the review workload the approach seeks to avoid.
+
+**Demonstration:** If one worker corrects the observer list and another adds a note against the same base, the store can combine those changes automatically. If both revise the same species entry, the overlap remains review work. Original submissions and the field-level sources of the combined result remain available.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Partial — Scientific coherence:** Conservative, scientifically informed rules can avoid many unsafe combinations, but every rule boundary is an assumption about which edits are independent. A schema-level disjoint change can still cross an unmodeled relationship between observations.
+- **Strong — Review workload and export readiness:** Unrelated edits become a current combined record without coordinator reconciliation, leaving only overlapping changes in the queue. Fewer visits need to be withheld from export for concurrency review.
+- **Weak — Implementation and maintenance burden:** The team must version, test, and maintain merge rules as scientific fields evolve, and must handle three-way list and species-entry semantics in addition to revision storage.
+- **Partial — Explainable provenance:** Field-level provenance can provide a detailed explanation, but the store and review interface must retain and present the base, changes, merge-rule outcome, and contributing submissions correctly.
+- **Partial — Offline sync cost:** Structured changes can reduce upload size, but tablets must retain base snapshots and reliable change descriptions, and retries must preserve that richer contract.
+
+**Summary:** Field-aware merging trades maintained scientific merge rules and richer provenance for a smaller review queue, more export-ready visits, and potentially smaller uploads.
+
+## Selection
+
+Use **whole-visit revisions**. Scientific coherence is the decisive criterion: automatically combining fields that are structurally unrelated can still combine observations that are scientifically related. Whole visits avoid that class of silent interpretation without requiring the record store to understand botany. Complete snapshots also provide the most direct provenance and keep reconciliation rules stable as the scientific schema evolves.
+
+This choice turns on **Do not silently discard a submitted edit**, **Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable**, and the preference for workflows that do not require specialized administration. It deliberately accepts a cost against review workload and export readiness: two coordinators must resolve concurrent edits even when a person would immediately judge them unrelated.
+
+Field-aware three-way merge remains a credible alternative rather than a rejected capability. It would deserve reconsideration if unresolved visits regularly jeopardize the Friday export, if observed conflicts are predominantly simple disjoint edits, or if coordinator time becomes a greater risk than merge-rule maintenance. Adopting it would require scientifically reviewed and versioned merge rules, regression cases for cross-field relationships, field-level provenance throughout review and export, and tablet support for base snapshots and structured changes.
+
+### Tablet-Sync Obligations
+
+Tablet-sync must accommodate the selected contract as follows:
+
+- Retain the exact base revision ID used for each edit and submit a complete visit payload with that base.
+- Preserve idempotency keys across restarts and retries. An original acceptance response is not proof of the latest visit state, so synchronization must retrieve current state when needed.
+- Treat a stale base as an accepted branch, not as a failed upload. Do not silently rebase the worker's changes onto a newer revision.
+- Represent both settled and unresolved outcomes. While unresolved, there is no current revision for the tablet to treat as the agreed visit.
+- Preserve the worker's complete submitted payload when displaying competing revisions; do not reduce the conflict to automatically selected fields.
+- Use the coordinator's result revision as the base for subsequent edits after resolution. A late edit based on older history can make the visit unresolved again and must remain visible.
+- Allow any authenticated worker to read and edit any visit, and permit complete-payload corrections to the plot UUID as well as other scientific fields.
+- Budget for full-visit uploads rather than depending on delta uploads or retaining base snapshots for server-side merging.
+
+### Review-Export Obligations
+
+Review-export must accommodate the selected contract as follows:
+
+- List unresolved visits and present every complete candidate revision with its author, base, and receipt metadata.
+- Provide comparison across complete revisions. A visual field diff may assist coordinators, but it must not imply that the server merged or selected individual fields automatically.
+- Let a coordinator produce a complete result revision, either by selecting a candidate unchanged or entering corrections.
+- Send the exact candidate set reviewed. On `REVIEW_SET_CHANGED` or `VISIT_NOT_UNRESOLVED`, retrieve the latest state and require review of any newly visible candidates rather than retrying the old outcome as a new decision.
+- Make resolution requests idempotent and distinguish replay of the original result from the visit's latest state.
+- Show resolution provenance linking the coordinator, all resolved candidates, and the result revision. Field-level merge provenance is not required because no automatic field merge occurs.
+- Exclude every unresolved visit from the concurrency-eligible export set. Only a settled visit's current revision is available to the export pipeline, subject to any additional review policy.
+- Size coordinator workflow and queue visibility for unrelated concurrent edits as well as true scientific conflicts; those visits cannot enter the CSV until resolved.
